<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # There Are So Many Types of Clubs! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> There Are So Many Types of Clubs!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Narrate a story or an experience in a structured way. Interactive: Negotiate project roles and tasks within a group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate a personal experience related to a club activity by including key details and a clear sequence of events (e.g., "Last year, I joined a sports club at school. First, I signed up during the club fair and then attended weekly practice sessions. During those meetings, I learned new skills and made new friends. Finally, we participated in a school tournament, and it was an unforgettable experience.").
                Can negotiate project roles in a group discussion about starting a club by suggesting real club positions and responsibilities (e.g., "Let's decide who will be the president to lead meetings, the secretary to take notes, and the treasurer to manage the club's budget.").
                Can express a preference for a club role and explain reasons (e.g., "I'd like to be the treasurer because I am good at math.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of target language and lesson objectives.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays images of various school clubs (e.g., a debate club, a photography club, a sports team, a coding club). Students work in pairs to discuss: "Which of these clubs looks most interesting to you? Why?" and "Have you ever joined a club? If yes, briefly tell your partner about your experience." This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to "clubs," "interests," and "activities." The teacher encourages the use of simple past for past experiences: "Last year, I joined..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models narrating a personal club experience, incorporating simple past and key vocabulary. For example: "Last year, I joined a debate club. First, I signed up during the club fair and then attended weekly meetings. During those meetings, I learned how to discuss and organize arguments. Finally, we participated in a school tournament, and it was an unforgettable experience." The teacher also models expressing a preference for a role and negotiating: "I'd like to be the president because I am good at leadership and can manage meetings." The teacher explicitly highlights the pronunciation of verbs ending in -ate like "participate," "negotiate," "delegate," "coordinate" (stress on the third syllable from the end) and contrastive sounds /pr/ in "project" and /tr/ in "treasure." The teacher emphasizes the imperative in "set goals!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Last year, I joined a debate club,' am I talking about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: "The past.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we talk about 'negotiating roles,' are we just telling people what to do, or are we discussing and agreeing together?" (Expected answer: "Discussing and agreeing together.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording or short dialogue script (e.g., a "podcast excerpt" or "recorded conversation") between two students, Ana and Ben, planning to start a new "Community Service Club" at school. The dialogue includes: expressing interests, suggesting types of activities, setting goals, and discussing potential roles and responsibilities. It uses simple present, simple past, imperatives, and target vocabulary (e.g., 'clubs,' 'interests,' 'activities,' 'goals,' 'members,' 'responsibilities,' 'roles,' 'meetings,' 'team,' 'project,' 'organize,' 'create,' 'plan,' 'manage,' 'negotiate,' 'social,' 'collaborative').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening for specific information to identify club interests, activities, and potential roles. This input directly supports the speaking outcomes by providing examples of how to express preferences, narrate experiences, and negotiate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen/read the dialogue.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What kind of club do Ana and Ben want to start?" (Expected answer: A Community Service Club)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students mark statements about the dialogue as True or False. E.g., "Ana wants to be the treasurer." (False) "Ben suggests that they should plan weekly meetings." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match suggested club roles (e.g., president, secretary, treasurer) with their responsibilities mentioned in the dialogue (e.g., 'lead meetings,' 'take notes,' 'manage budget').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking through structured exercises.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing narrating personal experiences using simple past; expressing preferences using simple present; using imperatives for giving instructions/suggestions; practicing pronunciation of target sounds and word stress. Focus on vocabulary: 'clubs,' 'interests,' 'activities,' 'goals,' 'members,' 'responsibilities,' 'roles,' 'meetings,' 'team,' 'project,' 'manage,' 'negotiate,' 'plan,' 'organize,' 'create,' 'discuss,' 'participate,' 'assign,' 'delegate,' 'social,' 'structured,' 'organized,' 'collaborative,' 'responsible.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or a handout. Students complete them orally in pairs, focusing on correct grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Last year, I joined a ______ club, and I ______ new skills." (e.g., sports, learned)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I'd like to be the ______ because I am good at ______." (e.g., treasurer, math/managing budgets)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To start the club, we need to ______ goals and ______ activities." (e.g., set, plan)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors pronunciation, specifically /pr/, /tr/ and stress on -ate verbs.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice - Dialogue Reconstruction:</b> Students receive jumbled parts of short dialogues about starting a club or narrating an experience. In pairs, they arrange them into a logical conversation.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What type of club would you like to join?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I'd like to join a photography club. I think it would be very creative."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "That's a great idea! Who should manage the equipment?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I can take that responsibility because I like to organize things."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages students to substitute words and phrases, paying attention to the pronunciation of "organize" and "responsibility."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "My Club Experience & Role Preference":</b> Students interview each other using prompts: "Tell me about a club you've joined or would like to join. What activities did you do/would you do? How was the experience?" and "If you were to start a new club, what role would you like to have and why?" Students must use simple past for narration and simple present for preferences. They are encouraged to include details like 'First, then, during, finally.'</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Role-play and Discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 per group).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario:</b> Each group acts as a "Club Founding Committee" responsible for creating a brand new school club based on shared "interests."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 1 (5 min): Brainstorming & Club Idea:</b> Groups discuss "There are so many types of clubs!" and choose one unique club idea (e.g., a "sustainable gardening club," a "digital storytelling club," a "board game enthusiasts club"). They briefly explain what the club is about, its "goals," and primary "activities" using simple present. (e.g., "Our club focuses on recycling. We plan events to clean up the community.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 2 (7-8 min): Negotiating Roles & Responsibilities:</b> Within their groups, students must "negotiate" and "assign" the following "roles": President, Secretary, Treasurer, and Activities Coordinator. Each student must express a preference for a role and provide a reason, while also responding to others' suggestions. They must use imperatives to suggest actions and plans. (e.g., "I'd like to be the treasurer because I am good at math and can manage the club's budget well." "Okay, let's assign Maria as secretary so she can take notes during meetings. You should coordinate events, Juan.") Encourage using verbs like "delegate," "coordinate," "facilitate," "support."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 3 (2 min): Group Spokesperson:</b> Each group designates a spokesperson to briefly present their club idea and the assigned roles to the class, possibly narrating any interesting discussions or "feedback" from their "negotiate" session (using simple past).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, task completion (negotiating roles), and demonstrating the ability to narrate reasons and suggestions. Correct pronunciation, especially of "-ate" verbs and stressed imperatives, is encouraged.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during Stage 4. The teacher will circulate, listen to group discussions, and use a checklist to assess individual student performance based on the evaluation criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' active participation in the group discussion and negotiation of roles during the "Let's Start a Club!" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency (Speaking):</b> Can express ideas clearly with some hesitation but without significant communication breakdown.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Successfully uses simple present for preferences/descriptions, simple past for narration, and imperatives for suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates relevant vocabulary (e.g., 'roles,' 'responsibilities,' 'manage,' 'negotiate,' 'plan,' 'participate') accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction and Negotiation:</b> Can suggest roles, express preferences, and respond to group members' feedback and suggestions effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Generally intelligible, with effort to pronounce contrastive sounds and emphasize stress on verbs ending in -ate.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> On a small slip of paper or digital form, students answer:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What role did you prefer in your group's club, and what was one reason you gave?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "Name one new word or phrase you used today to negotiate or describe your club."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What was challenging about narrating your club experience or negotiating roles, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "How will practicing negotiating roles and responsibilities be useful for you outside of English class?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of narrating experiences and negotiating roles did students find easy/difficult?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the pronunciation points (e.g., -ate verbs, contrastive sounds) successfully integrated and practiced? What adjustments are needed for the next lesson?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the speaking activities allow for sufficient use of simple past, simple present, and imperatives?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with expressing reasons or responding to feedback?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), students will consolidate the ideas discussed today by writing a formal proposal for their club, outlining its structure, goals, and the assigned roles and responsibilities. This will reinforce the vocabulary and grammar used in speaking, transitioning to written production.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>